---
import Journey from '../../layouts/Journey.astro';
import Step from '../../components/journey/Step.astro';
import More from '../../components/journey/More.astro';
import Next from '../../components/journey/Next.astro';
import ForLevel from '../../components/level/ForLevel.astro';
import Term from '../../components/journey/Term.astro';
import LessonRef from '../../components/journey/LessonRef.astro';
import CopyPrompt from '../../components/site/CopyPrompt.astro';
import Fact from '../../components/data/Fact.astro';
import Verified from '../../components/data/Verified.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/nav';
// The stop lengths below are the made-up ones in journey/chapters/jobs.ts (STOP, OPEN_THREAD).
const facts = ['t3code.worktrees', 't3code.shortcuts.workspace', 't3code.shortcuts.background', 't3code.limited', 't3code.queueSteer', 't3code.worktreeFolder'];
---
<Journey chapter="jobs" first="one" title="Several jobs" description="Running several agent jobs at once, in one project or in several, with one of you to answer them.">
  <Step id="one" title="While it works, you wait">
    <p>One <Term id="job" /> is one agent loop from <LessonRef id="loop" />, here on the club’s member list. Watch the readout: across a made-up hour you spend most of it waiting.</p>
    <ForLevel is={['technical', 'engineer']}><p>In <LessonRef id="parallel" /> helpers split one task. Here each job is separate, with its own end.</p></ForLevel>
  </Step>
  <Step id="three" title="Start another">
    <p>Each job runs in its own <Term id="thread" />, one conversation with one agent, and its own copy of the project, a <Term id="worktree" />. Each copy sits on its own <Term id="branch" />, a line of changes that joins main when the job is done.</p>
    <ForLevel is={['technical', 'engineer']}><p>By hand: <code>git worktree add ../club-search -b search</code>, one per job; each branch merges into main on its own after the type check and tests.</p></ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="engineer"><p>The worktrees share one repository’s history; each needs its own dependencies, its own dev server port and, if the app has one, its own test database.</p></ForLevel>
    <More title="Three at once in T3 Code">
      <ol>
        <li>One thread per job. <Fact id="t3code.shortcuts.workspace" /> opens the workspace menu: pick a new worktree so the job gets its own copy and branch.</li>
        <li>Send the brief with <Fact id="t3code.shortcuts.background" />: the thread runs in the background and a fresh composer opens for the next job.</li>
        <li><Fact id="t3code.worktrees" show="claim" /> Merge into main one job at a time.</li>
      </ol>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="many" title="There is still one of you">
    <p>Push it to five. A bare “Done.” means you read the work yourself, about eight made-up minutes; proof and one short question take two.</p>
    <div class="controls">
      <label class="control"><span>Jobs at once <output>5</output></span>
        <input type="range" min="1" max="5" step="1" value="5" data-bind="n" />
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="pick" aria-label="What reaches you">
      <button type="button" data-do="stop" data-val="long" data-default aria-pressed="true">“Done.”</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="stop" data-val="short" aria-pressed="false">Proof and short questions</button>
    </div>
  </Step>
  <Step id="projects" title="One project or several">
    <p>In one project, jobs share one main: when two touch the same page, the later one is sent back to update first. In several projects nothing collides, and each has its own instruction file you switch between.</p>
    <ForLevel is="engineer"><p>One repository: one history, one merge order, one CI. Several: separate histories, separate secrets and settings, and nothing that checks they still fit together.</p></ForLevel>
    <div class="pick">
      <button type="button" data-do="projects" data-val="one" data-default aria-pressed="true">One project</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="projects" data-val="many" aria-pressed="false">Several projects</button>
    </div>
  </Step>
  <Step id="board" title="Keep the tray readable">
    <p>Each job has a card on <Term id="tray">the tray</Term>. A thread called “fix” has to be opened before you know what it wants; a name, a state and what it needs saves those minutes at every stop.</p>
    <div class="pick" aria-label="How a card reads">
      <button type="button" data-do="board" data-val="vague" data-default aria-pressed="true">A thread called “fix”</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="board" data-val="clear" aria-pressed="false">A name, a state, what it needs</button>
    </div>
    <More title="Do this">
      <p>Start each job in a new worktree, name its thread after the job, and end every brief with this:</p>
      <CopyPrompt compact title="Ending for every brief">When you stop, start with one line: done, blocked, or a question for me. If done, say which tests you ran and what they printed, and attach a screenshot of anything visible. If a question, ask one question I can answer in a sentence.</CopyPrompt>
      <p><Fact id="t3code.queueSteer" show="claim" /> <Fact id="t3code.limited" show="claim" /></p>
    </More>
    <Next from="jobs" more={[{ href: 'wiki/parallel-work/', text: 'Parallel work', side: true }, { href: 'wiki/worktree/', text: 'Worktrees', side: true }]} />
  </Step>
  <Fragment slot="foot">
    <p>The hour, the jobs and the minutes per stop are made up to show the mechanism.</p>
    <Verified ids={facts} />
  </Fragment>
</Journey>
